   201	11. Disconnect the YubiKey immediately after each approved operation.
   202	
   203	Touch confirms that a person is present. Touch does not confirm which archive the YubiKey signs. The approval steps
   204	remain necessary.
   205	
   206	## 8. Key ceremony (once)
   207	
   208	Rehearse the whole ceremony with a throwaway key first (see "Rehearsal").
   209	
   210	1. Start a signing session. Set the GnuPG home to a temporary folder in memory.
   211	2. Generate the release key (OpenPGP, ECDSA P-256). Write its full fingerprint on paper.
   212	3. Make the two encrypted backups. In file mode, also make the encrypted primary medium.
   213	4. List each intended persistent copy by a non-secret label.
   214	5. In hardware mode, load the key into the YubiKey and configure it (see "YubiKey configuration").
   215	6. Export the public key to the output medium. Never export a secret key to a transfer medium.
   216	7. Delete the temporary exports and keyring. Stop the GnuPG agent. Detach every protected medium.
   217	8. Shut down the session.
   218	9. For each backup, start a new session with a new temporary keyring. Restore that backup only.
   219	10. Compare the full fingerprint with the paper. Make a test signature and verify it.
   220	11. In a separate session, test the selected primary in the same way.
   221	12. A cached key or unlocked agent must never stand in for the copy under test.
   222	
   223	Persistent copies after the ceremony:
   224	
   225	- Hardware mode: the YubiKey and the two encrypted backups only.
   226	- File mode: the encrypted primary medium and the two encrypted backups only.
   227	- None of these media can be used for transfer, boot or operational storage. None can touch the NUC.
   228	
   229	The session runs in memory, so it creates no plaintext secret on a disk. Do not describe ordinary file deletion as
   230	proven erasure. *(Author: if any persistent residue cannot be avoided, state the selected method.)*
   231	
   232	Public-key bootstrap:
   233	
   234	1. On a device independent of the NUC, compute the full fingerprint of the exported public key.
   235	2. Compare it with the paper.
   236	3. After publication, check the actual key and fingerprint in each place: the freeze record, the verifier
   237	   documentation and the user documentation.
   238	4. Keep the approved public key and fingerprint outside the NUC.
   239	5. Give users one authenticated way to get the fingerprint that does not pass through the NUC, the repository or
   240	   DNS. *(Author: name it, or state authenticated first acquisition as an assumption with a concrete delivery
   241	   method.)*
   242	
   243	Two matching web publications alone do not authenticate the release key.
   244	
   245	Custody record: write a dated record with the fingerprint, the custody mode, the procedure revision and hash, the
   246	signing setup, the laptop credential check, and confirmation that the backups are in two places. Do not record
   247	where they are. Link the rehearsal record.
   248	
   249	## 9. Each release
   250	
   251	Preparation on the NUC:
   252	
   253	1. The owner instance must prepare the verifier release and its trust file.
   254	2. It must write a release summary that lists every change from the last approved release.
   255	3. It must include the SHA-256 hash of the release archive.
   256	4. The author must check the actual release through the independent approval steps below.
   257	
   258	The summary does not replace those steps.
   259	
   260	Independent approval in the review VM:
   261	
   262	1. The author must keep the approved baseline outside the NUC.
   263	2. For the first release, the author must create the baseline independently. The first NUC summary is not the
   264	   baseline. *(Author: say how you create it.)*
   265	3. Check the actual candidate source and trust file against the baseline.
   266	4. Use a trusted, version-pinned release checker. *(Author: name the checker and its pinned hash.)*
   267	5. The checker must check the existing release invariants: unchanged entries and pins, nondecreasing `as_of`,
   268	   version plus one, the `previous_sha256` chain, and no retroactive close of an open end.
   269	6. If the service-key list is adopted, the checker must also check the complete initial schedule, non-overlap of
   270	   windows, and `not_before` later than the previous `as_of`.
   271	7. Reject every unexplained change, including deletions and changes to verifier code.
   272	8. Check each new service-key fingerprint and window against the authorization record.
   273	9. A match with the two authority records must never authorize an entry.
   274	10. For other new trust entries, record their non-secret source.
   275	11. Build the reviewed source in the review VM.
   276	12. Check that the built verifier contains the approved trust-file hash and the frozen catalog binding.
   277	13. Record the approved hashes: the archive, the trust file and the catalog.
   278	14. Record the author's decision. Write the approved archive and the public key to a new input medium.
   279	
   280	*(Author: say how you obtain each service-key fingerprint without the NUC and the two authority records.)*
